step 1. 
    street safety data in nyc

step 2.
    dataset Name: nyc crashes data
    organization: nyc crash mapper
    url: crashmapper.org 
    date accessed: 10/11/26
    description: interactive webpage to visualize transportation collisions in the city. meant to advocate for safer streets and traffic measures. publicly available data
    time period covered: 2011-2026 ('24-'26 for this assignment)
    geocoverage: nyc, from boroughs down to nta blocks
    # of rows: 50,824
    # of columns: 25
    file format: csv
    license and terms of use: extension of nyc open data, freee and open for public use

step 3. 
    important variables: person involved in crash (driver, motorist, cyclist, pedestrian), injured or killed, neighborhood attributes (address, borough), contributing factors
    numerical: number of [injured or killed], crash counts
    categorical: street name, contributing factors, vehicle types
    time data: date_time
    geo variables: lat, long, geom, cartodb, ~ website has geojson file export option
    interesting variables: address, types of crashes, contributing factors
    missing or unsual: some null values, not comprehensive since not DOT data, non-uniform data entries
    potential limitations: maybe have to clean up/uniform data, vehicle types column is not uniform at all

    data from summary stats below

step 4.
    what is the highest cause of accidents in nyc?
    what type of mobility is most at risk in nyc?
    which borough see the most amount of accidents?
    can i see which intersections are the most dangerous?

step 5.
    showing type of accidents boroughs are dealing with
    what type of vehicles are most at risk in the city
    original interest came from trying to visualize what streets might be ripe to made safer through street design interventions
    final result: intersection data to specfically find streets to "fix" didn't work out as planned, would need more time to explore this dataset and find a workaround without dot's more specific data since that is public, might have to be a more intense mapping project


